                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Feels Like 1992, minus the \"You Have Died\" screen! 🧝‍♂️\r\n\r\nAs someone who spent my childhood hoarding saves in King’s Quest V and Loom, playing The Order of the Thorne: The King's Challenge on Steam felt like a warm, pixelated hug. Infamous Quests perfectly captured the iconic 320x200 VGA aesthetic and unironic, gentle fantasy tone of Sierra's golden era.\r\n\r\nThe Nostalgia FactorTrue Sierra Atmosphere: The gorgeous hand-painted backdrops look like they were pulled right out of an old DOS box, even offering built-in CRT monitor scanline options!\r\n\r\nThe Bard’s Lute: Instead of standard inventory combinations, solving puzzles by learning and strumming songs on your lute is a phenomenal mechanic that heavily respects LucasArts' Loom.\r\n\r\nFairer Game Design: Unlike Roberta Williams' old games, you cannot permanently lock yourself out of winning, and you won’t die unexpectedly just for clicking on the wrong tree.\r\n\r\nAchievements & Bugs (The Veteran's Breakdown)\r\n\r\nAn Easy 100%: If you are a trophy hunter, you will love this. The Steam Achievements are highly attainable in a single, thorough 4-hour playthrough.\r\n\r\nKickstarter Bloat: Some achievements require you to read nearly 40 tedious backer tombstones at the Isle of the Honored or flip through random backer-written library books, which feels a bit like artificial padding.\r\n\r\nEngine Quirks: Watch your clicks! The game engine has a known scripting bug where opening your lute menu while closely zoomed into the swamp's spiderweb completely hard-locks the game.\r\n\r\nAudio Mix Faults: While the narrator is quite fun, some character dialogue sounds like it was recorded on a cheap $10 headset, clashing hard with the pristine, beautiful musical score.\r\n\r\nFinal Verdict\r\n\r\nIt is a short but incredibly sweet fairytale. If you want to bypass the stress of massive modern RPGs and relive the simple joy of point-and-click pixel hunting, it is worth every penny.\r\n\r\nScore: 7.5 / 10",
